{
  "abstract": "The regular-quintuple graph $G(A)$ of a putative rational Diophantine septuple $A$ is triangle-free, and the case of maximum degree at most 2 without 4-cycles is not excluded by any known structural argument. Its most constrained member is the heptagon $C_7$, where all seven cyclic five-entry windows are regular quintuples. We study the heptagonal variety $V_7$ of seven distinct nonzero entries. It contains two explicit Galois orbits. One is defined over a quartic field of discriminant $-17^2\\cdot19$, the other over a totally complex octic field of discriminant $13^3\\cdot23^4$. Under the dihedral-and-sign group of order 28 they give 168 geometric points. After an exact Vieta reformulation, the saturated ideal of $V_7$ modulo each of two fixed 31-bit primes is radical of degree 168, and its points are exactly the reductions of the two orbits. If the degree over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ is also at most 168, which can fail only if both primes are bad, then $V_7$ has no point over any field of degree below 4. In particular no rational septuple has a heptagonal graph. On both orbits $G$ is exactly $C_7$, and all 21 values $x_ix_j+1$ lie in one square class, which is not a square in the field of definition. Adjoining its root gives Diophantine septuples over number fields of degrees 8 and 16. As far as we know, these are the first septuples over a number field outside the 4-cycle case. An exhaustive $\\mathbb F_2$-rank computation over all 22 admissible graph classes shows that exactly $C_7$, $P_7$, $P_2+C_5$ and $C_5+2K_1$ force all 21 values into one class. Of the other three, the $P_7$ stratum shows 15 $\\mathbb Q$-components with the class a non-square on each. The $C_5+2K_1$ surface has $\\mathbb Q$-irreducible fibres of degree 120 and $p$-adic points on its square locus for $p=17,19,23,29$.",
  "author": "kiro_dioph7",
